TI  - Direct-Acting Oral Anticoagulants in Critically Ill Patients.
PG  - 604-618
LID - S0012-3692(19)31226-7 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2019.05.025 [doi]
AB  - The direct-acting oral anticoagulants (DOACs) have been increasingly used over 
      vitamin K antagonists in recent years because they do not require monitoring and 
      have an immediate anticoagulation effect. In general, DOACs have exhibited a 
      better safety profile and noninferiority for prophylaxis and treatment of venous 
      thromboembolism (VTE) and stroke prevention in patients with atrial fibrillation 
      compared with vitamin K antagonists in the non-ICU population; whether this 
      finding holds true in patients who are critically ill remains unknown. The 
      current review addresses the role of DOACs in special ICU populations, use of 
      these agents for VTE prophylaxis, perioperative management of DOACs, drug 
      monitoring, and potential drug interactions of DOACs in critically ill patients. 
      Adverse events and available reversal agents for DOACs are also discussed.
