TI  - Leading Change and Negotiation Strategies for Division Leaders in Clinical 
      Medicine.
PG  - 1246-1253
LID - S0012-3692(19)31312-1 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2019.06.019 [doi]
AB  - Most physician leaders assume their administrative role based on past 
      achievements but with very little leadership training. In this article, leaders 
      of the Association of Pulmonary, Critical Care, and Sleep Division Directors 
      describe two leadership skills that are often required to effectively lead in a 
      clinical division at an academic or community hospital setting: leading change 
      and negotiation strategy. We adopted our discussion from the business sector and 
      refined the approaches through our own experiences to help division leaders in 
      leading a successful team, whether as a division chief, residency or fellowship 
      program director, or a clinical service director. Leading any change project may 
      include an eight-step process, starting with creating a sense of urgency and 
      completing with anchoring the change to the organizational culture. We then 
      review negotiation strategies, comparing positional bargaining vs principled 
      negotiation, to create more changes and continuing growth for the division. 
      Finally, we discuss the importance of emotional intelligence, exemplary 
      leadership practices, and self-development that the division leader should 
      embrace.
