TI  - ED Door-to-Antibiotic Time and Long-term Mortality in Sepsis.
PG  - 938-946
LID - S0012-3692(19)30157-6 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2019.02.008 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: The impact of antibiotic timing on sepsis outcomes remains 
      controversial due to conflicting results from previous studies. OBJECTIVES: This 
      study investigated the association of door-to-antibiotic time with long-term 
      mortality in ED patients with sepsis. METHODS: This retrospective cohort study 
      included nontrauma adult ED patients with clinical sepsis admitted to four 
      hospitals from 2013 to 2017. Only patients' first eligible encounter was 
      included. Multivariable logistic regression was used to measure the adjusted 
      association between door-to-antibiotic time and 1-year mortality. Secondary 
      analyses used alternative antibiotic timing measures (antibiotic initiation 
      within 1 or 3 h and separate comparison of antibiotic exposure at each hour up to 
      hour 6), alternative outcomes (hospital, 30-day, and 90-day mortality), and 
      alternative statistical methods to mitigate indication bias. RESULTS: Among 
      10,811 eligible patients, median door-to-antibiotic time was 166 min 
      (interquartile range, 115-230 min), and 1-year mortality was 19%. After 
      adjustment, each additional hour from ED arrival to antibiotic initiation was 
      associated with a 10% (95% CI, 5-14; P < .001) increased odds of 1-year 
      mortality. The association remained linear when each 1-h interval of 
      door-to-antibiotic time was independently compared with door-to-antibiotic time ≤ 
      1 h and was similar for hospital, 30-day, and 90-day mortality. Mortality at 1 
      year was higher when door-to-antibiotic times were > 3 h vs ≤ 3 h (adjusted OR, 
      1.27; 95% CI, 1.13-1.43) but not > 1 h vs ≤ 1 h (adjusted OR, 1.26; 95% CI, 
      0.98-1.62). CONCLUSIONS: Delays in ED antibiotic initiation time are associated 
      with clinically important increases in long-term, risk-adjusted sepsis mortality.
