TI  - A 63-Year-Old Woman With Pulmonary Micronodules and Chronic Cough.
PG  - e47-e50
LID - S0012-3692(19)30829-3 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2019.03.034 [doi]
AB  - A 63-year-old woman presented with a 2-year history of nonproductive cough. She 
      denied the presence of shortness of breath, chest pain, arthralgia, muscle 
      weakness, weight loss, night sweats, and fatigue. She was a never smoker and had 
      a history of arterial hypertension and diabetes. There was no history of asthma, 
      allergic rhinitis, nasal polyps, or gastroesophageal reflux disease, known 
      malignancy, or collagen tissue disease. She reported exposure to house mold. 
      There was no family history of respiratory diseases. The patient denied alcohol 
      consumption, illicit drug use, occupational exposures, any recent travel, or 
      exposure to TB.
