TI  - Sleep and Nocturnal Gastroesophageal Reflux: An Update.
PG  - 963-971
LID - S0012-3692(18)30813-4 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2018.05.030 [doi]
AB  - Nocturnal gastroesophageal reflux has been associated with poor sleep quality. 
      Normal physiological adaptations of the aerodigestive system to sleep prolong and 
      intensify nocturnal reflux events. This occurrence leads to sleep disruption, as 
      well as to esophageal, laryngeal, and laryngopharyngeal reflux. Controversy 
      exists on whether OSA and nocturnal reflux are causally linked or merely 
      associated because of shared risk factors. Advances in diagnostic technology have 
      provided new insights into gastroesophageal reflux and the mechanisms of 
      nocturnal reflux during sleep. This update reviews new data on causal links 
      between sleep and gastroesophageal reflux disease.
