TI  - A 26-Year-Old Man With a Pleural Effusion and Headache.
PG  - e113-e117
LID - S0012-3692(18)30582-8 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2018.04.011 [doi]
AB  - A 26-year-old man presented to the ED with dizziness and gait imbalance. He noted 
      dizziness for 3 months, but symptoms progressed more rapidly over the last 
      3 weeks when he began experiencing nausea, vomiting, and "wobbly legs." These 
      symptoms would worsen with physical exertion, especially when lifting heavy 
      objects. On review of systems, he also reported subjective fevers and chills. He 
      had no appreciable dyspnea, cough, chest pain, or increased sputum production. 
      The patient recalled a history of "brain surgery" approximately 4 years prior to 
      his presentation, but no further details could be provided at the time.
