TI  - A 79-Year-Old Woman With Dyspnea and Hypoxemia That Worsened in an Upright 
      Position.
PG  - e139-e142
LID - S0012-3692(17)31249-7 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2017.07.004 [doi]
AB  - A 79-year-old woman presented to the ED with complaints of gradually worsening 
      exertional dyspnea, dizziness, and chest discomfort. For several weeks she had 
      not been able to perform light household work. The patient's medical history 
      mentioned pulmonary embolism following immobilization (2012), several fractures 
      after trauma, an ischemic cerebral vascular accident (2014), and curative 
      treatment for breast cancer (1995). Her current medication included esomeprazole, 
      clopidogrel, simvastatin, calcium/vitamin D, amitriptyline, and acetaminophen.
