TI  - Extracorporeal Membrane Oxygenation for Adult Respiratory Failure: 2017 Update.
PG  - 639-649
LID - S0012-3692(17)31089-9 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2017.06.016 [doi]
AB  - The use of extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO) for respiratory failure in 
      adults is growing rapidly, driven in large part by advances in technology, which 
      have made ECMO devices easier to implement and safer and more efficient. 
      Accompanying this increase in use is a nearly exponential increase in 
      ECMO-related literature. However, the great majority of the literature is 
      composed of retrospective observational data, often in the form of single-center 
      studies with relatively small numbers of subjects. The overall lack of 
      high-quality data, including prospective randomized trials, makes it difficult to 
      justify the rate at which ECMO use is increasing and calls attention to the need 
      for more rigorously designed studies. Nonetheless, given its ability to support 
      patients with severe gas exchange impairment and the potential for it to minimize 
      the deleterious effects of invasive mechanical ventilation, there appears to be a 
      legitimate role for ECMO in severe respiratory failure in adults.
