DP  - 2016 Dec
TI  - A 40-Year-Old Woman With Back Pain.
PG  - e159-e165
LID - S0012-3692(16)58629-2 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2016.08.1449 [doi]
AB  - A 40-year-old woman consulted our ED for a 7-month history of left dorsal back 
      pain and dyspnea. The pain was initially dull and mechanical. Her general 
      practitioner started nonsteroidal antiinflammatory drugs and physiotherapy, which 
      provided partial relief. One week before consulting, the intensity of the pain 
      increased, and she started to feel shortness of breath when performing her daily 
      activities. She had lost 5 kg during the previous month. The patient was a 
      healthy woman who lived in an urban area of Barcelona, Spain. She did not smoke 
      or take drugs of abuse, and she worked as a butcher. During the initial 
      evaluation, her blood pressure was 131/76 mm Hg, heart rate was 120 beats/min, 
      temperature was 36.2°C, and ambient air pulse oximetry was 98%.
