TI  - An Official Critical Care Societies Collaborative Statement-Burnout Syndrome in 
      Critical Care Health-care Professionals: A Call for Action.
PG  - 17-26
LID - S0012-3692(16)01269-1 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2016.02.649 [doi]
AB  - Burnout syndrome (BOS) occurs in all types of health-care professionals and is 
      especially common in individuals who care for critically ill patients. The 
      development of BOS is related to an imbalance of personal characteristics of the 
      employee and work-related issues or other organizational factors. BOS is 
      associated with many deleterious consequences, including increased rates of job 
      turnover, reduced patient satisfaction, and decreased quality of care. BOS also 
      directly affects the mental health and physical well-being of the many critical 
      care physicians, nurses, and other health-care professionals who practice 
      worldwide. Until recently, BOS and other psychological disorders in critical care 
      health-care professionals remained relatively unrecognized. To raise awareness of 
      BOS, the Critical Care Societies Collaborative (CCSC) developed this call to 
      action. The present article reviews the diagnostic criteria, prevalence, 
      causative factors, and consequences of BOS. It also discusses potential 
      interventions that may be used to prevent and treat BOS. Finally, we urge 
      multiple stakeholders to help mitigate the development of BOS in critical care 
      health-care professionals and diminish the harmful consequences of BOS, both for 
      critical care health-care professionals and for patients.
