TI  - An 80-Year-Old Man With Dyspnea and Bilateral Pleural Effusions After Partial 
      Nephrectomy for Renal Cell Carcinoma.
PG  - e147-50
LID - S0012-3692(15)00414-6 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2015.12.023 [doi]
AB  - An 80-year-old man presented because of superficial head trauma sustained after 
      falling from bed. On review of systems, he reported worsening dyspnea 
      on exertion, nonproductive cough, and weight loss over the preceding 2 to 
      3 months. There was no report of chest pain or leg swelling. He had a past 
      medical history of hypertension, coronary artery disease, subclinical 
      hypothyroidism, and renal cell carcinoma treated with partial right nephrectomy 
      approximately 1 year before this presentation. Two months earlier he had been 
      evaluated in the dermatology clinic for painful, dystrophic fingernails. At that 
      time he was diagnosed with acropachy with onycholysis and suspected 
      superinfection, and after failing to improve with vinegar soaks and topical 
      antimicrobials, he underwent surgical nail removal on the second and fourth 
      digits of the right hand. Histological examination of the operative specimens 
      revealed dystrophic nails with negative fungal stains. His medications included 
      levothyroxine, hydrochlorothiazide, and clopidogrel. He had never smoked and had 
      done clerical work until retirement. He was originally from Colombia.
