DP  - 2015 Mar
TI  - Bardet Biedl syndrome: motile ciliary phenotype.
PG  - 764-770
LID - S0012-3692(15)39674-4 [pii]
LID - 10.1378/chest.13-2913 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Cilia line the surface of the respiratory tract and beat in a 
      coordinated wave to protect the lungs against infection. Bardet Biedl Syndrome 
      (BBS) is a rare condition attributed to cilia dysfunction. Murine models of BBS 
      suggest a respiratory phenotype; however, no reports have studied the translation 
      of these findings in patients. METHODS: We assessed the clinical symptoms of 
      motile cilia dysfunction and the histology of ciliated respiratory epithelium in 
      patients with BBS. RESULTS: We report an increased prevalence of neonatal 
      respiratory distress at birth (12%), general practitioner-diagnosed asthma (21%), 
      otitis media (33%), and rhinitis (36%) in patients with BBS. These symptoms, 
      however, occurred at a significantly reduced prevalence compared with patients 
      with known motile cilia dysfunction (primary ciliary dyskinesia). Respiratory 
      epithelial assessment revealed cellular damage, significant ciliary depletion (on 
      60% of ciliated cells), and goblet cell hyperplasia in patients with BBS (50% 
      goblet cells). These findings were quantifiably similar to those of patients with 
      asthma (P > .05). Surprisingly, motile cilia function and ultrastructure were 
      grossly normal with the exception of occasional unique inclusions within the 
      ciliary membrane. CONCLUSIONS: In conclusion, motile ciliary structure and 
      function are essentially normal in patients with BBS.
