TI  - High-Frequency Oscillatory Ventilation in Adults With ARDS: Past, Present, and 
      Future.
PG  - 1306-1317
LID - S0012-3692(17)31185-6 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2017.06.025 [doi]
AB  - High-frequency oscillatory ventilation (HFOV) is a unique mode of mechanical 
      ventilation that uses nonconventional gas exchange mechanisms to deliver 
      ventilation at very low tidal volumes and high frequencies. The properties of 
      HFOV make it a potentially ideal mode to prevent ventilator-induced lung injury 
      in patients with ARDS. Despite a compelling physiological basis and promising 
      experimental data, large randomized controlled trials have not detected an 
      improvement in survival with the use of HFOV, and its use as an early 
      lung-protective strategy in patients with ARDS may be harmful. Nevertheless, HFOV 
      still has an important potential role in the management of refractory hypoxemia. 
      Careful attention should be paid to right ventricular function and lung stress 
      when applying HFOV. This review discusses the physiological principles, clinical 
      evidence, practical applications, and future prospects for the use of HFOV in 
      patients with ARDS.
