TI  - A 65-year-old man with persistent cough and large nodular opacity.
PG  - e13-e17
LID - S0012-3692(15)30266-X [pii]
LID - 10.1378/chest.14-1172 [doi]
AB  - A 65-year-old Asian man with a history of chronic hepatitis B infection presented 
      to our pulmonary clinic for second opinion of his chronic, persistent, 
      nonproductive cough. He was evaluated 10 months earlier with chest CT scan, which 
      revealed a large lingular nodular opacity that was diagnosed as nodular 
      cryptogenic organizing pneumonia by CT scan-guided percutaneous lung biopsy. 
      Systemic corticosteroids were initiated and continued over the next 10 months. 
      The dry cough persisted, and he developed intermittent left-sided pleuritic chest 
      pain. He denied fevers, night sweats, hemoptysis, weight loss, or dyspnea. He was 
      a lifelong nonsmoker and moved to the United States from China during childhood.
