TI  - A 40-Year-Old Man With Albinism and Progressive Dyspnea.
PG  - e143-e146
LID - S0012-3692(18)30815-8 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2018.05.032 [doi]
AB  - A 40-year-old male subject employed as a grocery store manager presented to a 
      pulmonary clinic with a dry cough and progressive dyspnea of 1 year duration. The 
      patient was previously an avid cyclist and first noted his dyspnea when he was 
      unable to bike as far as before. Bilateral interstitial lung infiltrates were 
      recently noted on chest radiography. At the time of presentation, he could no 
      longer cycle due to dyspnea. The patient's medical history was significant for 
      albinism and severe visual impairment. He had no family history of albinism or 
      pulmonary disorders. He had never smoked, drank alcohol only occasionally, and 
      had no significant environmental exposures.
