TI  - Oral Appliances for the Management of OSA: An Updated Review of the Literature.
PG  - 544-553
LID - S0012-3692(17)31069-3 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2017.06.005 [doi]
AB  - Oral appliances (OAs) are becoming increasingly recognized not only as an 
      alternative to but also possibly as an adjunct treatment modality for OSA. 
      Compared with CPAP, the gold standard therapy, OAs are less efficacious but are 
      more accepted and tolerated by patients, which, in turn, may lead to a comparable 
      level of therapeutic effectiveness. Different OA designs currently exist, and 
      more are constantly emerging. Additionally, state-of-the-art technologies are 
      being used in the fabrication of many; however, all the currently available OAs 
      employ the same mechanism of action by targeting the anatomical component 
      involved in the pathogenesis of the disease. Furthermore, the scope of use of OAs 
      is expanding to include patients who are edentulous. For patients with OAs, the 
      dentist is a member of an interdisciplinary team managing OSA, and constant 
      communication and follow-up with the sleep physician and other team members is 
      necessary for disease management.
